nge of national leader.","source":"REIGN leader list","source_url":"https://oefdatascience.github.io/REIGN.github.io/","historical":false,"contested":null,"refs":null},{"iso3":"RUS","date":"1991-08-21","year":1991,"kind":"regime_change","title":"Regime becomes parliamentary","detail":"Regime classification from Geddes, Wright & Frantz, as distributed with REIGN.","source":"REIGN regime list","source_url":"https://oefdatascience.github.io/REIGN.github.io/","historical":false,"contested":null,"refs":null},{"iso3":"RUS","date":"1992-01-01","year":1992,"kind":"derived_democratic_shift","title":"Democratic opening: electoral democracy index rose 0.35 over 5 years","detail":"From 0.14 in 1987 to 0.50 in 1992. Derived by detecting a sustained shift of at least 0.2 index points over 5 years; marked at the steepest year of the episode.","source":"derived: V-Dem electoral democracy index","source_url":null,"historical":false,"contested":"V-Dem indices are aggregated expert judgements with published uncertainty intervals. A shift of this size is well outside the noise, but the exact year is a property of the detection rule, not a documented event.","refs":null},{"iso3":"RUS","date":"1992-01-01","year":1992,"kind":"derived_economic_rupture","title":"Output collapse: GDP per capita fell 15%","detail":"Real GDP per capita fell from 7,432 to 6,346 (constant 2015 US$) in a single year.","source":"derived: World Bank GDP per capita","source_url":null,"historical":false,"contested":"A one-year collapse of this size usually has several causes at once - war, currency crisis, commodity price, or the end of a political order. This marker says it happened; it does not say why.","refs":null},{"iso3":"RUS","date":"1992-01-02","year":1992,"kind":"economic_reform","title":"Shock therapy - price liberalisation","detail":"Most administered prices were freed at once, followed by mass privatisation through voucher auctions from 1992 to 1994.","source":"curated","source_url":null,"historical":false,"contested":"Output fell sharply and life expectancy dropped through the 1990s. Defenders argue the collapse was inherited from the Soviet system and that partial reform would have been captured by insiders; critics argue speed itself destroyed institutions and handed assets to a narrow group. This is one of the most heavily contested episodes in modern economic history.","refs":"[\"Russian Federation, Presidential Decree on liberalisation of prices, December 1991\", \"Stiglitz, 'Globalization and Its Discontents' (2002)\", \"\\u00c5slund, 'How Russia Became a Market Economy' (1995)\"]"},{"iso3":"RUS","date":"1993-04-01","year":1993,"kind":"election","title":"Referendum election","detail":"Vote, Early","source":"REIGN election list","source_url":"https://oefdatascience.github.io/REIGN.github.io/","historical":false,"contested":null,"refs":null},{"iso3":"RUS","date":"1993-09-21","year":1993,"kind":"regime_change","title":"Regime becomes personal","detail":"Regime classification from Geddes, Wright & Frantz, as distributed with REIGN.","source":"REIGN regime list","source_url":"https://oefdatascience.github.io/REIGN.github.io/","historical":false,"contested":null,"refs":null},{"iso3":"RUS","date":"1993-12-01","year":1993,"kind":"election","title":"Referendum election","detail":"Vote, Early","source":"REIGN election list","source_url":"https://oefdatascience.github.io/REIGN.github.io/","historical":false,"contested":null,"refs":null},{"iso3":"RUS","date":"1994-01-01","year":1994,"kind":"derived_economic_rupture","title":"Output collapse: GDP per capita fell 13%","detail":"Real GDP per capita fell from 5,799 to 5,072 (constant 2015 US$) in a single year.","source":"derived: World Bank GDP per capita","source_url":null,"historical":false,"contested":"A one-year collapse of this size usually has several causes at once - war, currency crisis, commodity price, or the end of a political order. This marker says it happened; it does not say why.","refs":null},{"iso3":"RUS","